A||||9||fieroc4||washer fluid on windshield||||5||
Z||000000||fieroc4||01-29-2000||08:41 AM||||Hey guys!<P>Any suggestions for this annoyance?  When I make a nice right (you know the kind, toss it in to 2nd and swoop around at about 30) I get  a "spitting" of washer fluid on the windshield, just on the left side.  My first thought was the switch was going bad in the column so I unplugged the power to the washer motor, but it still happens.<P>Dan||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||01-29-2000||08:56 AM||||Has it been precipitating lately?  I don't know much about this, but maybe you have some water around your windshield and this is where the water/fluid is coming from.  Maybe when you make a hard turn one side of your windshield squeezes the frame.  Just a thought.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wrench||01-29-2000||08:57 AM||||I get the same thing. It is caused by inertia. Solution is to slow down or spped up after the turn to blow the water off!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||01-29-2000||12:06 PM||||The line runs horizontaly between the spray heads. It's the fluid in that section of line being spit up. The only way, maybe, to fix it would be rerout the lines.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick||01-29-2000||04:27 PM||||<BR>The same thing happens to my Fiero.<P>I read somewhere that there is a problem with the <B>cap</B> on the Fiero's windshield washer reservoir not venting properly. What happens is that pressure builds up in the reservoir (from heat) and forces fluid into the line. The most common complaint is that the fluid level continually drops on it's own. I suppose that under certain conditions, this fluid is also forced to spray onto the windshield. ||||reg||3
Z||000005||fieroc4||01-30-2000||02:41 AM||||Wrench & Patrick<BR>Thank you, I thought I was nuts, it's good to know this isn't just my problem.  Yes, I always speed up to blow the water off, how else could you get it off the glass :-) ?<P>I will check the vent on the cap, I had thought of that but it seemed too far fetched...<P>Dan||||reg||1
Z||000006||fieroc4||02-03-2000||02:55 AM||||Update:  It was the cap not venting properly.  I just drilled a small (3/32) hole just below the top of the filler, and no more splash.<P>Thanks for the help.<BR>Dan||||reg||1
Z||000007||Boxdin||02-03-2000||06:56 PM||||I have the identical problem on my 88 Formula ! Thanks for the solution already.||||reg||6
Z||000008||86SE||02-03-2000||08:59 PM||||I got it!!<BR>Put glue (or duct tape) on the little hole where the fluid comes out...<P>||||reg||3
Z||000009||Patrick||02-03-2000||09:41 PM||||<BR><B>86SE</B>, I don't think you quite understand.<P>The washer fluid is being forced out the spray nozzles. The "little hole" you're referring to is probably the vent hole. By plugging the vent hole you would be making the situation worse.||||reg||1
